1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:14,990
Life isn‘t fair.
生活是不公平的。

2
00:00:14,990 --> 00:00:20,530
Some people just seem blessed with the ability to effortlessly charm anyone they meet.
有些人似乎就是拥有一种能力，能够毫不费力地吸引他们遇到的每一个人。

3
00:00:20,530 --> 00:00:27,830
You know the kind of person, the one who can strike up a conversation with a complete stranger and end up with a new friend or business lead.
你知道那种人，就是那种能够和一个完全陌生的人开始一段对话，最后还能结交到新朋友或者获得商业线索的人。

4
00:00:27,830 --> 00:00:29,080
How do they do it?
他们是怎么做到的呢？

5
00:00:29,080 --> 00:00:34,960
Well, it might seem like a magical power, but in fact there are a number of factors at work.
嗯，这可能看起来像是一种神奇的力量，但实际上有许多因素在起作用。

6
00:00:34,960 --> 00:00:38,420
So, can you learn to develop superhuman charm?
那么，你能学会培养出超凡的魅力吗？

7
00:00:38,420 --> 00:00:41,840
The answer is: to a large extent, yes you can.
答案是：在很大程度上，你可以。

8
00:00:41,840 --> 00:00:43,640
But first, the bad news.
但首先，有个坏消息。

9
00:00:43,640 --> 00:00:49,130
Scientists have found that people initially judge each other based purely on physical appearance.
科学家们发现，人们最初纯粹是根据外貌来评判彼此的。

10
00:00:49,130 --> 00:01:01,290
With just a fleeting glimpse of a face, people make snap judgements about each other’s likeability, trustworthiness and confidence, according to Alexander Todorov, professor of psychology at Princeton University.
普林斯顿大学的心理学教授亚历山大·托多罗夫表示，仅仅通过匆匆瞥过一张脸，人们就能对彼此的好感度、可信度和自信程度做出快速判断。

11
00:01:01,290 --> 00:01:03,150
How to counteract this?
如何抵消这种情况呢？

12
00:01:03,150 --> 00:01:06,680
There‘s one incredibly simple tool: your smile.
有一个极其简单的办法：你的微笑。

13
00:01:06,680 --> 00:01:13,740
Todorov told the BBC that people perceive a smiling face as “more trustworthy, warmer and sociable”.
托多罗夫告诉英国广播公司，人们认为一张微笑的脸“更值得信赖、更温暖且更善于社交”。

14
00:01:13,740 --> 00:01:15,850
It sounds like common sense, doesn’t it?
这听起来像是常识，不是吗？

15
00:01:15,850 --> 00:01:18,470
Smile and others will smile with you.
你微笑，别人也会对你微笑。

16
00:01:18,470 --> 00:01:21,240
What other tricks might we have up our sleeves?
我们还可能有哪些窍门呢？

17
00:01:21,240 --> 00:01:26,380
Former FBI agent Jack Schafer has been trained in how to influence people.
前联邦调查局特工杰克·谢弗接受过如何影响他人的培训。

18
00:01:26,380 --> 00:01:33,020
He told BBC Capital: “Our brains are always surveying the environment for friend or foe signals.”
他告诉BBC资本频道：“我们的大脑总是在审视周围环境，寻找是朋友还是敌人的信号。”

19
00:01:33,020 --> 00:01:42,160
Three things we can do to signal that we are not a threat are to: raise our eyebrows quickly, tilt our heads slightly, and, once again, to smile.
为了表明我们没有威胁，我们可以做三件事：快速扬起眉毛、微微倾斜头部，还有，再次强调，微笑。

20
00:01:42,160 --> 00:01:49,910
So we‘ve looked at body language, but of course what you say is hugely important too, unless you want to just stand there grinning foolishly.
所以我们已经探讨了肢体语言，但当然，你说的话也非常重要，除非你想只是站在那里傻乎乎地咧嘴笑。

21
00:01:49,910 --> 00:01:57,180
Here Schafer recommends that “the golden rule of friendship is if you make people feel good about themselves, they’re going to like you.”
谢弗在这里建议说：“友谊的黄金法则是，如果你能让人们自我感觉良好，他们就会喜欢你。”

22
00:01:57,180 --> 00:02:03,340
In other words, you need to show interest in them, instead of talking about yourself and all your wonderful achievements.
换句话说，你需要表现出对他们的兴趣，而不是谈论你自己和你所有了不起的成就。

23
00:02:03,340 --> 00:02:09,780
And while you‘re chatting, remember this: another way of showing interest is to mirror their physical position.
而且当你聊天的时候，记住这一点：另一种表现出兴趣的方式是模仿他们的身体姿势。

24
00:02:09,780 --> 00:02:11,610
Another way to form a connection?
另一种建立联系的方式呢？

25
00:02:11,610 --> 00:02:12,900
Find common ground.
寻找共同点。

26
00:02:12,900 --> 00:02:25,080
Suzanne de Janasz, a professor of management with Seattle University, says that charming people are particularly adept at seeking out shared interests or experiences to help them build rapport.
西雅图大学的管理学教授苏珊娜·德·亚纳兹说，有魅力的人特别擅长找出共同的兴趣或经历，以此来帮助他们建立融洽的关系。

27
00:02:25,080 --> 00:02:31,900
Simple things like asking where someone‘s from really can open up a discussion and allow you to find areas in common.
像问问别人来自哪里这样简单的事情，真的可以开启一场讨论，让你找到共同之处。

28
00:02:31,900 --> 00:02:37,070
And if all else fails, you can fall back on that most British of topics: the weather.
而且如果其他方法都行不通，你还可以求助于最具英国特色的话题：天气。

29
00:02:37,070 --> 00:02:38,820
Glorious day, isn’t it?
今天天气真好，不是吗？
